+::a42a1 Every contribution carries a small magnifier symbol that leads to the integrity page of its debate. ::a42a2 That page lists the fingerprint of every commit, names the signing key of the platform, hands out the whole repository as a git clone, and gives details on how to check fingerprints and signatures. ::a42a3 The repository carries the same instructions in its own README, so a copy of it explains itself.
+
+::a42a4 The short answer is that you clone the repository once and run `git pull --ff-only` whenever you come back. ::a42a5 New contributions arrive without comment; a rewritten history makes the command refuse and say so. ::a42a6 Rewriting is not automatically an attack, since contributions occasionally have to be removed for organisational or legal reasons, but it is never invisible, and such an event is meant to be announced and explained on the integrity page.
